\subsection{Optical Absorption}

Figure\,\ref{fig:optic} summarises the optical absorption of all four phases in the independent-parcticle approximation and in the presence of excitonic effects. For the $Immm$ phase in Fig.\,\ref{fig:optic}(a), the inclusion of excitons leads to a strong optical absorption peak around 1.9\,eV, below the band gap for polarisation along $z$, agreeing well with previous photoemission studies\,\cite{Weaver1991}. The peaks for polarisation along the in-plane directions are mainly above the band gap, and have stronger absorption. For the $R\bar{3}m$ phase, the in-plane polarised light has a strong absorption peak slightly below 1.9\,eV, as shown in Fig.\,\ref{fig:optic}(b). For the van der Waals fcc $Fm\bar{3}$ and $Pa\bar{3}$ phases, the strongest absorption peaks are above the band gap, almost reaching $1.5\times10^5$\,cm$^{-1}$. 

