    \textbf{Datasets.}
    We conduct experiments on two such datasets: Bongard OpenWorld~\citep{wu2024bongardopenworld} and Bongard Human-Object-Interaction (HOI)~\citep{jiang2022bongardhoi}.
    The OpenWorld dataset was divided into train (500), validation (100), and test (500) sample splits, ensuring distinct semantic components across them.
    For HOI, we utilized its original splits, where 4000 balanced samples across concepts were chosen from train split.
    Its validation ($4 \cdot 100$) and test sets ($4 \cdot 200$) balanced samples, which are categorized by concept novelty: seen/unseen object/action.
    Each sample consisted of 6 positive images, 6 negative images and 1 query image.

    \textbf{Models.}
    Baseline performance was established on multiple SoTA VLMs: Phi 3.5 vision 4.2B~\citep{abdin2024phi3}, Pixtral 12B~\citep{agrawal2024pixtral},
    Gemma3 4B and 27B~\citep{team2025gemma}, InternVL3 14B~\citep{chen2024internvl}, and Qwen 2.5 VL 7B and 72B~\citep{bai2025qwen25vl}.
    Subsequently, we applied PEFT to Gemma3 4B, Phi and Pixtral.

    \textbf{Evaluation.}
    For all fine-tuning experiments, we saved model checkpoints every epoch and selected the one with the best performance on the validation set.
    Baselines and best-performing checkpoints were evaluated on their respective test splits.
    Models demonstrate consistent performance across all conceptual novelty splits (see \refAppendix{sec:hoi_split_results}), so we report the average for brevity.

