    \section{Linear reasoning bottleneck}
    \label{subsec:baseline_analysis}

    Our framework, applied to 8 SoTA VLMs across two datasets and tested with 8 prompt formats each, reveals a widespread \textbf{linear reasoning bottleneck}.
    As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:lsc_benchmark}, models' generative performance ($acc_{gen}$) struggles to surpass their LSC.
    Full results are in \refAppendix{sec:separability_ceiling}.

    \begin{figure}[htbp]
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{bongard_images/LSC_benchmark}
        \caption{generative accuracy vs. linear separability for all baseline models.
        The diagonal dashed line is the LSC benchmark ($y=x$).
        Points above the line indicate that a model's generative performance has surpassed its LSC.
        The plot shows that most evaluations struggle to consistently surpass the benchmark, demonstrating a prevalent linear reasoning bottleneck.}
        \label{fig:lsc_benchmark}
    \end{figure}

    \textbf{Pathways to success and failure.}
    However, some models surpass the LSC, revealing distinct success pathways.
    For instance, Pixtral 12B and Qwen2.5-VL 72B consistently achieve a generative performance statistically superior to their LSC\@.
    In contrast, other models like Gemma3 27B fail to do so reliably.
    Table~\ref{tab:model_comparison} highlights the dramatically different mechanisms behind these outcomes.

