    Our analysis reveals two pathways to surpass the LSC. The first, \textbf{non-linear representation refinement},
    is exemplified by Pixtral, which transforms its initial embeddings into a more linearly separable format ($acc_{final} > LSC$).
    The process suggests that next-token prediction itself can function as a representation learner,
    enhancing separability in a way that shares deep theoretical underpinnings with contrastive learning~\citep{shadennaifalshammari2025icon}.
    While its final generative accuracy is not a direct readout of these embeddings, its success is characterized by a strong
    statistical alignment between its generative output and the classifications from a linear probe on these improved final representations.

    The second observed route is \textbf{post-representation non-linear reasoning pathway},
    where the LLM remaps the initial visual embeddings into a more complex representational geometry ($acc_{final} < LSC$).
    Gemma3 27B is an extreme example of this, where its final embeddings collapse to chance-level separability ($acc_{final} \approx 50\%$)
    and its performance is brittle across different prompts.
    In contrast, Qwen2.5-VL 72B provides a stable and highly effective implementation of this pathway.
    Although its final representation accuracy also drops relative to its LSC, the model achieves consistent, SoTA performance.
    This suggests Qwen successfully leverages a sophisticated non-linear reasoning mechanism that can operate on these less-separable embeddings.
    This pathway highlights that a high LSC is not sufficient on its own; success depends on a robust reasoning process that can navigate non-linear representational spaces.

    \textbf{The disconnect between separability and generation.}
    Generally, better linear separability in a model's vision embeddings (LSC) correlates with better generative performance.
    A $\chi^2$ test shows a statistically significant dependence between the LSC probe and generative performance in 97 out of 128 instances with it being an inverse dependency only for 2 instances.
    A deeper look, however, reveals a complex disconnect between embedding separability and generative output.
    While model's processing attunes its final representations to its generative logic (significant in 103 out of 128 instances), the relationship is often counterintuitive,
    with 29 instances showing a significant \emph{inverse} dependence.
    The model’s reasoning can therefore be disconnected from, or even opposed to, the logic of a linear probe on the same embeddings.
    While high final separability is not a prerequisite for success, this suggests a hypothesis for creating more robust and interpretable models:
    train them to improve the linear discriminability of their final embeddings while ensuring that structure is positively aligned with the generative process.


    \section{Surpassing the LSC}\label{sec:peft}

    Our baseline analysis in Section~\ref{subsec:baseline_analysis} revealed a widespread linear reasoning bottleneck where VLMs struggle to surpass their LSC.
    Through targeted fine-tuning, we systematically probe intervention points within the VLM, from the vision-language interface to the LLM’s reasoning pathways, to pinpoint and resolve the bottleneck.

    \subsection{Experimental setup}\label{subsec:peft_setup}

    To pinpoint the most effective intervention points, we employ several parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) and minimal tuning methods.
    Each is trained with two distinct objectives to assess its impact on generative accuracy and representation quality.
    Full hyperparameters for all methods are detailed in \refAppendix{sec:tuning_details} and \refAppendix{sec:contrastive_tuning}.
    \begin{itemize}[noitemsep,topsep=0pt,leftmargin=*]
        \item[-] \textbf{Interface adaption:} To test if the LSC can be surpassed by minor adjustments at the modality interface, we tune isolated components: either the \emph{vision-language projection layer} or an additive \emph{vision bias vector} on initial patch embeddings.
        \item[-] \textbf{Prompt tuning:} By adding trainable soft prompts to the input sequence while keeping the VLM's core weights frozen, we test whether activating dormant reasoning pathways is enough when provided with an optimal input prompt.
        \item[-] \textbf{LoRA:} We adapt the model by injecting trainable, low-rank matrices into the attention layers of both the vision encoder and language model. This evaluates how adapting core weights can surpass the linear reasoning bottleneck and improve abstract reasoning.
    \end{itemize}

    \textbf{Objectives.} We train the PEFT parameters using two objectives.
    \textbf{Next-token prediction} ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{NT}}$), the standard language modeling objective of maximizing the likelihood of the ground-truth output tokens.
    And \textbf{InfoNCE + NTP} ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{combined}}$) objective, which augments $\mathcal{L}_{\text{NT}}$ with an explicit contrastive loss ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{sim}}$) by means of a weighted sum.
    $$ \mathcal{L}_{\text{combined}} = w_n \mathcal{L}_{\text{NT}} + w_c \mathcal{L}_{\text{sim}} $$
    This contrastive term explicitly encourages the final embeddings of a query image to be closer to its true category centroid than the alternative.
    The motivation is to directly improve the linear separability of the final representations, thereby raising the separability ceiling for the task while co-optimizing for generative correctness,
    which is further detailed in \refAppendix{sec:combined_loss_details}.

    \newpage

    \textbf{Evaluation.} To evaluate structural robustness, we test performance on unseen prompt formats.
    The in-distribution (ID) evaluation uses the standard interleaved prompt structure seen during training.
    For the out-of-distribution (OOD) test, we use a labeled prompt structure that groups all images by category at the end of the prompt.
    This OOD setup challenges how well the optimization targets generalize beyond the syntactic structure on which they were trained.
    Furthermore, OpenWorld test set consists entirely of new concepts unseen in train set, verifying conceptual generation.

