    \section{Separability ceiling in VLMs}
    \label{sec:separability_ceiling}

    The Table~\ref{tab:combined_baseline_performance} (next page) presents our baseline results, comparing each model's generative accuracy to its LSC ($acc_{vision}$) and its final representation accuracy ($acc_{final}$).

    While generative performance is not lower than their LSC in 84 out of 128 instances, only a handful of models manage to surpass this ceiling.
    This success is achieved through two distinct mechanisms.
    In 4 instances, models like Pixtral use \textbf{non-linear representation refinement}, where the model's processing increases the linear separability of the embeddings ($acc_{final} > LSC$).
    More commonly, in 10 instances, models like Qwen-VL-72B employ a \textbf{post-representation non-linear pathway}, where generative performance exceeds the LSC despite a decrease in the linear separability of the final embeddings ($acc_{final} < LSC$).

    A $\chi^2$ test highlights a complex relationship between embedding separability and generative output.
    The initial vision embeddings (LSC) show a statistically significant dependence on generative performance in 97 out of 128 instances, with only 2 of those being an inverse relationship.
    This suggests that the initial visual features are generally, and logically, aligned with the final output.
    However, the connection becomes more complicated after the model's reasoning process.
    While the final embeddings have a significant dependence on the output in 103 instances, a surprising 29 of these are an \textbf{inverse dependence}.
    This underlines the disconnect between simple linear discriminability and the complex, non-linear reasoning happening within these models.


