{\em Robust against observational uncertainties.--}
Although the GRB observed rate remains uncertain and modeled using normal distributions to capture the uncertainties (Eq.~\ref{eq:grbgwlikelihood}), the inference of $k_2$ remains largely robust across a broad range of assumptions, from $\rsgrb = 30$ to $2000$ Gpc$^{-3}$yr$^{-1}$ and $\eta$ values spanning 0.01 to 3. These variations mainly affect $k_1$ and $\mtov$ due to the steeper slope of the total mass distribution near and below $\mvl$. Modifications to $k_1$ and $\mtov$ have a stronger impact on the sGRB rate and the rate ratio.

We note that the observed GW merger rate uncertainty is already incorporated in our results (Eq.~\ref{eq:grblikelihood}).

{\em Agnostic to model.--}
We explore how the choice of our criteria affects the results by varying the parameters that are not inferred, i.e., parameters other than $(k_1,k_2,k_3,\mtov)$. 

Considering uncertainties in the mass ratios needed to induce significant torque on a deformed NS in BNS mergers, we allow the lower mass ratio threshold, $q_{\rm low}$ to range between 1.15 to 1.35 \citep{2019ARNPS..69...41S}. Similarly, we permit variation in the mass ratio required for substantial disk formation following NSBH mergers, with the upper threshold, $q_{\rm high}$ ranging from 2 to 10 \citep{2012PhRvD..86l4007F}. When we add up the GW merger rates within the mass range of interest, we find these choices of cut in mass ratio only affect less than 20\% of mergers with $\mtot>\mtov$. As a result, different parameter choices have no substantial impact (see Table~\ref{tab:ktable}).

NS remnants with $ M < \mvl $ would form a very long-lived NS. Although recent studies have proposed magnetars as possible progenitors of kilonova-associated lGRBs \citep{2022Natur.612..232Y,2025NSRev..12..401S,2025ApJ...978L..38C}, observational evidence strongly disfavors magnetar-driven GRB scenarios due to the lack of significant rotational energy injection signatures \citep{{2014MNRAS.437.1821M,2016ApJ...819L..22H,2020ApJ...902...82S,2021ApJ...920..109B}}. Therefore, we have assumed a minimum total mass $\mvl$ corresponding to the minimum mass for forming a meta-stable NS that would produce a sGRB.
The fraction of GW mergers with $\mtot<\mvl$ (say, if we assume $\mtov=2.2\msun$ and $k_1=1.15$) is less than 20\%. If we completely remove the restriction for a minimum total mass and assume all mergers with $\mtot<\mls$ are sGRBs, we do not find any significant difference (see Table~\ref{tab:ktable}). 

In summary, although the choices of mass cuts at the boundaries (e.g., light systems, heavy systems with low or high mass ratio) have not yet been fully explored theoretically, our results appear agnostic.

% \section{Discussion}
{\em Discussion.--} In this paper, we introduce a new approach to determine the relation between nuclear characteristic masses for NSs by comparing the GW and GRB observations. Our method offers an independent constraint on neutron star nuclear properties, complementary to existing techniques. The results rely on the assumption that merger-induced lGRBs and sGRBs are mainly divided by the total mass of the mergers. The relations appear to be insensitive to the large observational uncertainties and agnostic to other mass-related assumptions. Our findings highlight the necessity of further confirmation and study of the relation between merger-induced GRBs and their total mass.


Interestingly, \cite{2024arXiv241207846P} found a transition of merger short-lived remnant to long-lived remnant to lie between 1.3\,$\mtov$ and 1.4\,$\mtov$ from numerical relativity simulations. The transition found in the simulations is consistent with our results based on GW and GRB observations. Both results indicate a long lifetime after merger for relatively heavy NSs.

We find a strong correlation between $(k_2,k_3)$ and an anti-correlation between $(k_2,\mtov)$. Improved rate measurements of GW and GRB events will help further tighten these relations. Furthermore, independent determination of any of the characteristic masses $\mtov$, $\mls$, or $\mpc$ can simultaneously place constraints on the other two. For example, joint detections of GRB and BNS or NSBH mergers in GWs will connect the total merger mass to the GRB duration, providing an estimate for $\mls$, which can in turn be used to constrain $\mtov$ and $\mpc$.    

Our Bayesian framework ensures proper consideration of observational and theoretical uncertainties and reveals correlations between different characteristic NS masses. Although we focus on the total mass in this paper, our framework could also be extended to include other schemes, such as the dependency on the merger mass ratio.





