%
% stochastic optimal control & avoid the classifier guidance problem
Stochastic optimal control (SOC) methods \cite{nusken2021solving, hua2024simulation, domingo-enrich2024stochastic} have recently been explored to fine-tune pretrained diffusion models for a variety of downstream tasks \cite{uehara2024fine, berner2024an, wang2025finetuning, domingo-enrich2025adjoint}. These approaches steer the diffusion sampling process towards desired target rewards by incorporating an auxiliary stochastic controller into the drift term of the stochastic differential equation (SDE) governing the generative/reverse diffusion dynamics. \citet{uehara2024fine} further established a connection with classifier-based guidance, where SOC reward functions are defined by classifiers trained on clean data resources, which are often readily available across many domains. It thus enables SOC-guided sampling to be more advantageous when leveraging off-the-shelf classifiers directly. However, computing the optimal control value requires backpropagating reward signals through entire neural-SDE sampling trajectories \cite{tzen2019neural, uehara2024fine}, which presents a significant drawback restricting the scalability and practicality of SOC-based methods.
% connection to xT guidance to bypass simulation & training free
To circumvent the computational bottleneck, some recent works have proposed directly approximating the diffusion posterior through Tweedie's formula \cite{robbins1992empirical}. This avenue has been primarily explored in the context of general inverse problems \cite{chung2023diffusion, moufad2025variational}, and image diffusion finetuning applications \cite{yu2023freedom, ma2024elucidating, rout2025rbmodulation, janati2025mixture, dinh2025representative}. These methods are also often referred to as training-free diffusion guidance, as they rely solely on off-the-shelf classifiers rather than additional training. In addition, they alleviate the reliance on expensive SOC simulation-based inference for each step of the reverse diffusion process. 
% Tweedie suboptimal approximation
However, our work demonstrates that the direct posterior approximation via Tweedie’s formula is inherently suboptimal. By deriving a new variational lower bound on the value function of the SOC problem, our theoretical analysis reveals that the approximate posterior needs additional correction steps to remain aligned with the true distribution.

% contribution summary & soc low density & stein corrector & molecular ood exp
Here, we summarize our contributions: i) we propose a novel low-density diffusion guidance approach formulated under stochastic optimal controls by introducing a novel cost-to-go function, ii) we theoretically prove that approximating the diffusion posterior by the Tweedie-based proposal distribution is inferior and requires further correction steps, iii) we introduce a novel Stein correction mechanism for SOC-augmented diffusion sampling, which utilizes Stein variational inference tools \cite{liu2016stein, liu2017stein} to iteratively minimize the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between the approximate and true posterior distributions. Our experimental results suggest that the proposed Stein correction mechanism is crucial in improving training-free diffusion guidance for molecular sampling problems in low-density regions, leading to the discovery of molecules with higher binding affinities to target proteins.

\section{Preliminaries}
%
\subsection{Diffusion models}
% general statement & and reverse diffusion sdes to match soc (time direction 0->T)
\citet{song2021maximum} proposed a unified diffusion-model framework over continuous-time, $\forall t \in [0, T]$, and continuous-state space, $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^d$, utilizing a pair of forward and backward stochastic differential equations (SDEs). The forward process perturbs clean i.i.d data samples, $\mathbf{x}_T \sim p_T$, towards a predefined prior, $\mathbf{x}_0 \sim p_0$. Its dynamic is the solution to an Itô SDE: $d\mathbf{x}_t = \mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x}_t, t)dt + \sigma(t)d\mathbf{w}$, where $dt < 0$ is an infinitesimal timestep, $\mathbf{w}$ is the Weiner process, and $\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x}_t, t), \sigma(t)$ denote the drift and diffusion coefficients, respectively. Specific noise schedules $\mathbf{b}, \sigma$ can recover standard discrete-time diffusion frameworks, termed as variance-exploding \cite{song2019generative} and variance-preserving \cite{ho2020denoising} SDEs. A backward process gradually denoises samples from the prior $p_0$ and back to the data distribution $p_T$; the reverse dynamic corresponds to another Itô SDE of the form:
%
\begin{align}
\label{eq:rsde}
    \mathbb{P}: \;\;\; d\mathbf{x}_t = \left(-\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x}_t, t)+\sigma(t)^2\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_t}\log p_t(\mathbf{x}_t)\right)dt + \sigma(t)d\mathbf{w}
\end{align}
%
where the marginal data score, $\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_t}\log p_t(\mathbf{x}_t)$, can be estimated by the score-matching technique \cite{hyvarinen2005estimation} via a time-dependent score-based network, $\mathbf{s}_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_t\right) \approx \nabla_{\mathbf{x}_t}\log p_t(\mathbf{x}_t)$; for simplicity, we omit the explicit $t$-dependence in the score model notation. Unlike \citet{song2021maximum}, we employ a positive infinitesimal reverse timestep to facilitate our theoretical development of novel diffusion guidance in subsequent sections.
% classifier guidance
In sampling, diffusion models can incorporate a classifier $r(\cdot)$ to guide samples toward regions with desired properties, a technique known as classifier-based diffusion guidance \cite{dhariwal2021diffusion}. The conditional score can be factorized into $\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_t}\log p_t(\mathbf{x}_t|y) \propto \nabla_{\mathbf{x}_t}\log p_t(\mathbf{x}_t) + \nabla_{\mathbf{x}_t} r(y|\mathbf{x}_t)$, which requires training the classifier $r(\cdot)$ on noisy data of multiple noise levels, $\mathbf{x}_t \sim p_t(\mathbf{x}_t|\mathbf{x}_T), \forall t \in [0, T]$.

\subsection{Stein variational inference}
% short intro
Variational inference methods approximate a complex target distribution $p$ using a simpler distribution $q \in \mathcal{Q}$, a set of tractable distributions, by solving the KL divergence minimization problem, $\arg\min_q D_{KL}\left(q \| p\right)$. Stein variational gradient descent (SVGD) \cite{liu2016stein, liu2017stein} is a nonparametric method that finds $q$ as a set of interacting particles and deterministically evolves them along a direction $\phi$, which most efficiently decreases the KL divergence. 
% lemma 3 KL minimization
By leveraging on Stein’s identity and kernelized Stein discrepancy (KSD), \citet{liu2016stein} proved that the steepest gradient descent direction problem can be solved in the unit ball of a reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS) $\mathcal{H}$; the lemma below summarizes their core result:
%
\begin{lemma}
\label{theo:stein}
 $-\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}D_{KL}\left(q \|p\right)$ steepest direction maximizes Stein discrepancy in the RKHS unit ball:
    \begin{align}
    \label{eq:kl}
        &\phi^* = \arg\max_{\phi \in \mathcal{H}} \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x} \sim q} \left[\text{trace}\left(\mathcal{A}_p\phi\left(\mathbf{x}\right)\right)\right] \;\; \text{st} \;\; \|\phi\|_{\mathcal{H}} \leq  1 \nonumber \\
        &\text{with} \;\; \mathcal{A}_p\phi(\mathbf{\mathbf{x}}) = \nabla_{\mathbf{x}} \log p(\mathbf{x})\phi(\mathbf{\mathbf{x}}) + \nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\phi(\mathbf{\mathbf{x}})
    \end{align}
\end{lemma}
%
where $\mathcal{A}_p$ is the Stein operator,  $\phi$ is the Stein class of the target density $p$. \citet{liu2016kernelized} solved the problem (\ref{eq:kl}) as KSD that yields $\phi^*(\mathbf{x}^i)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}^j \sim q(\mathbf{x})}\left[\nabla_{\mathbf{x}^j}\log p(\mathbf{x}^j)k(\mathbf{x}^i, \mathbf{x}^j) + \nabla_{\mathbf{x}^j}k(\mathbf{x}^i, \mathbf{x}^j)\right]$, where $k(\mathbf{x}^i, \mathbf{x}^j)$ is a RKHS kernel, e.g. Radial Basic Function (RBF) $k(\mathbf{x}^i,\mathbf{x}^j)=\exp{\left(-\frac{1}{h}\|\mathbf{x}^i - \mathbf{x}^j\|_2^2\right)}$. SVGD can approximate the intractable target density $p$ by gradually transporting a set of $N$ initial particles $\{\mathbf{x}_0^i\}_{i=0}^{N} \sim q_0$ along the direction $\phi^*$, which relies on the computable score $\nabla_{\mathbf{x}} \log p(\mathbf{x})$. The first term of $\phi^*$ represents a kernel-weighted gradient ascent direction that pushes the particles toward the high-density regions of the target $p(\mathcal{\mathbf{x}})$. The second term denotes the repulsive force that prevents the particles from collapsing into the local models of $p(\mathbf{x})$. In practice, the set of initial particles can be set arbitrarily, and the convergence of particles to their actual density can be guaranteed $\sum_{i=1}^Nh(\mathbf{x}^i)/N - \mathbb{E}_{q(\mathbf{x})}[h(\mathbf{x})]=\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{N})$ for bounded testing functions $h$ \cite{del2013mean}.

