\subsection{Stochastic optimal control}
% soc formulation
In general, the stochastic optimal control problem\cite{nusken2021solving, domingo-enrich2024stochastic} seeks an optimal controller that steers the behavior of a given stochastic system to minimize a pre-specified cost function. For the dynamical system of reverse diffusion process in Equation \ref{eq:rsde}, we formalize an affine-control problem as below:
%
\begin{align}
\label{eq:soc}
     &\inf_{\mathbf{u} \in \mathcal{U}} \mathbb{E} \left[ \int_t^T \left(\frac{1}{2} \|\mathbf{u}\left(\mathbf{x}_t^{\mathbf{u}},t\right)\|^2+ f\left(\mathbf{x}_t^{\mathbf{u}},t\right) \right)dt + g(\mathbf{x}_T^{\mathbf{u}}) \right] \nonumber \\ 
    &s.t. \;\;\;  \mathbb{P}^{\mathbf{u}}: \;\;\; d\mathbf{x}_t^{\mathbf{u}} = \left(-\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x}_t^{\mathbf{u}}, t)+\sigma(t)^2\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_t^{\mathbf{u}}}\log p_t(\mathbf{x}_t^{\mathbf{u}}) + \sigma(t)\mathbf{u}\left(\mathbf{x}_t^{\mathbf{u}}, t\right)\right)dt + \sigma(t)d\mathbf{w}
\end{align}
%
where $\mathbf{u}: \mathbb{R}^d \times [0, T] \mapsto \mathbb{R}^d$ denotes the feedback control that drives the system dynamics and takes the values from a set of admissible controls $\mathcal{U}$, $f: \mathbb{R}^d \times [t, T] \mapsto [0, \infty)$ specifies the intermediate state cost, $g: \mathbb{R}^d \mapsto [0, \infty)$ is the terminal cost, and $\mathbb{P}^u$ denotes the controlled probability path measures from $\mathbb{P}$. 
% cost & value functions
The control objective finds an optimal feedback that can minimize the cost functional: $J\left(\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{x}, t\right) = \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}^{\mathbf{u}}}\left[\int_t^T \left(\frac{1}{2} \|\mathbf{u}\left(\mathbf{x}_s^{\mathbf{u}},s\right)\|^2+ f\left(\mathbf{x}_s^{\mathbf{u}},s\right) \right)ds + g(\mathbf{x}_T^{\mathbf{u}}) | \mathbf{x}_t = \mathbf{x}\right]$. The minimum cost functional defines the value function or optimal cost-to-go \cite{fleming2006controlled}: $V\left(\mathbf{x}, t\right)= \inf_{\mathbf{u} \in \mathcal{U}} J\left(\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{x}, t\right)$.
%  lemma 1 solving HJB + Verification & FBSDES & Kac-Feyman soc for optimal controller
Moreover, the verification theorem \cite{fleming2006controlled, pham2009continuous} proves that the optimal control and value function relate to each other, $\mathbf{u}^* = -\sigma \nabla_{\mathbf{x}} V$. The affine stochastic control problem with a quadratic cost on the control can be closely connected to an iterative diffusion optimization (IDO) technique with a relative entropy (or KL divergence) loss \cite{powell2021reinforcement, kappen2012optimal, hartmann2012efficient}, which involves simulating multiple controlled trajectories, computing their cumulative costs, and backpropagating through the trajectories to update a parameterized controller. The value function then satisfies a Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman partial differential equation (HJBPDE) \cite{pavliotis2014stochastic} associated with the uncontrolled reverse SDE (\ref{eq:rsde}), which can be solved by means of the celebrated Feynman-Kac formula \cite{pham2009continuous}; a more extensive treatment of SOC problem can be found in \cite{nusken2021solving, domingo-enrich2024stochastic}. Here, we summarize the essential results in the following lemma:
%
\begin{lemma}
\label{theo:soc}
    Optimal controller and value function for the control problem in Equation \ref{eq:soc}:
    \begin{align}
    \label{eq:uv}
         &\mathbf{u}^*\left(\mathbf{x}, t\right) =  -\sigma(t) \nabla_{\mathbf{x}} V(\mathbf{x}, t)\nonumber\\
        &V(\mathbf{x}, t) = - \log \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}} \left[\exp\left(- \int_t^Tf\left(\mathbf{x}_s,s\right)ds - g\left(\mathbf{x}_T\right)\right)| \mathbf{x}_t=\mathbf{x}\right]
    \end{align}
\end{lemma}
%
where $\mathbf{x}$ refers to the solution of the uncontrolled problem (\ref{eq:rsde}). As we observe, at each state, we need to integrate through the diffusion trajectories from $\mathbf{x}_t=\mathbf{x}$ under $\mathbb{P}$ to get the value function, and compute the gradients $\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}V(\mathbf{x},t)$ within the simulation trajectories to output the optimal controller. These operations are very costly and significantly slow for practical applications. 
% state cost =0 & connection to classifier guidance
In some cases, the state cost is often being ignored $f(\mathbf{x}_t,t)=0 \; \forall t$, in which \citet{uehara2024fine} established the connection between SOC-based diffusion finetuning and classifier guidance. By using the single sample estimation, the optimal controller can be shown to be equivalent to $\mathbf{u}^* =  \sigma(t) \nabla_{\mathbf{x}} \log \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}} \left[\exp\left(- g\left(\mathbf{x}_T\right)\right)| \mathbf{x}_t=\mathbf{x}\right] \propto \nabla_{\mathbf{x}} \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}}\left[r(\mathbf{x}_T)|\mathbf{x}_t=\mathbf{x}\right]$
, wherein $r = -g$, due to the minimization problem, is an off-the-shelf classifier, which was trained from clean data, or a differential reward function.

\section{Stein diffusion guidance}
% short intro
We present a surrogate control objective to the original SOC problem. We begin discussing a novel SOC cost functional that enables low-density sampling with guided diffusion models. We then introduce a variational bound on the SOC's value function, by which we show that standard training-free guidance methods need additional correction steps on the clean diffusion manifold. We propose the Stein correction mechanism that relates to the optimal controller of the surrogate value function. Given the varying terminology in the existing literature, we use the terms classifier and reward function interchangeably throughout the sections below. We provide the detailed proofs in Appendix.

\subsection{Low density diffusion sampling as a stochastic control problem}
\label{sec:guid}
% short intro
We consider the controlled reverse SDE (\ref{eq:soc}) and its associated probability path measure $\mathbb{P}^{\mathbf{u}}$. We then propose a novel cost functional $J(\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{x}, t)$ that progressively anneals the marginal density $p_t(\mathbf{x}_t)$ of the uncontrolled diffusion process (\ref{eq:rsde}) under $\mathbb{P}$ to low-density regions. Following Lemma \ref{theo:soc}, we formulate the low-density diffusion guidance problem as a stochastic optimal control task:
% proposition low density SOC-guided sampling
\begin{proposition}
\label{theo:cost-func}
Let the cost functional of the control problem in Equation \ref{eq:soc} be $J\left(\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{x}, t\right) = \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}^{\mathbf{u}}}\left[\int_t^T \left(\frac{1}{2} \|\mathbf{u}\left(\mathbf{x}_s^{\mathbf{u}},s\right)\|^2+ \alpha(s)\log p_s\left(\mathbf{x}_s^{\mathbf{u}}\right) \delta(s-t) \right)ds - \beta(t) r(\mathbf{x}_T^{\mathbf{u}}) | \mathbf{x}_t = \mathbf{x}\right]$, then the annealed reward-guided marginal density $p_t^{\mathbf{u}}(\mathbf{x}_t)$, the value function $V\left(\mathbf{x}, t\right)$, and the optimal control  $\mathbf{u}^*\left(\mathbf{x}, t\right)$ corresponding to the controlled-reverse SDE under $\mathbb{P}^{\mathbf{u}}$ can be derived as follows:
\begin{align}
    &p_t^{\mathbf{u}}(\mathbf{x}_t)= p_t^{1-\alpha(t)}(\mathbf{x}_t)\exp(\beta(t)r(\mathbf{x}_T))\nonumber \\
    &\mathbf{u}^*\left(\mathbf{x}, t\right)=\sigma(t) \nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}}\left[\frac{p_t^{\mathbf{u}}(\mathbf{x})}{p_t(\mathbf{x})}|\mathbf{x}_t = \mathbf{x}\right] \;\; \text{and} \;\;
    V\left(\mathbf{x}, t\right) =  -\log \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}}\left[\frac{p_t^{\mathbf{u}}(\mathbf{x})}{p_t(\mathbf{x})}|\mathbf{x}_t = \mathbf{x}\right]
\end{align}
\end{proposition}
% comment on the annealed density
where  $\alpha(t)$ and $\beta(t)$ denote the annealing schedules controlling low-density weighting and guidance strength, respectively, $r(\cdot)$ is an off-the-shelf classifier, which was priorly trained on clean data samples, or a differential reward function. The induced marginal density is the product of the annealed density term $p_t^{1-\alpha(t)}$ and the guidance term $\exp(\beta(t)r(\mathbf{x}_T))$, which can be interpreted as an un-normalized energy density with the energy function $-\beta(t)r(\mathbf{x}_T)$.

% simulation-based &  Tweedie solution
As discussed previously, getting the SOC solutions is computationally expensive as one have to simulate at each reverse diffusion step $\mathbf{x}_t=\mathbf{x}$, following the uncontrolled reverse process $\mathbb{P}$, to obtain the endpoint samples $\mathbf{x}_T$ on the clean diffusion manifold $\mathcal{M}_T$, which are then evaluated by the differential classifier/ reward function $r(\mathbf{x}_T)$. Notably, one also needs to backpropagate through the sampling trajectories to get the optimal control $\mathbf{u}^*\left(\mathbf{x}, t\right)$ \cite{uehara2024fine, wang2025finetuning}. As a remedy to the expensive SOC simulation, we can approximate the diffusion posterior by a proposal distribution $q_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T | \mathbf{x}_t) \approx p_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T | \mathbf{x}_t)$, whose mean value can be estimated via Tweedie's formula $\frac{\mathbf{x}_t +\gamma^2(t) \mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_t, t)}{\eta(t)}$, assuming a forward perturbing kernel $p_{t|T}(\mathbf{x}_t | \mathbf{x}_T) = \mathcal{N}(\eta(t)\mathbf{x}_T, \gamma^2(t)I)$. 
% connect to next section
This one-step posterior approximation has been previously explored in many prior works. However, in this study, we argue that the Tweedie-based proposal distribution is inherently suboptimal. As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:arch}, the approximate solution can significantly deviate from the endpoint sample given by the SOC simulation. In the following sections, we present an upper variational bound on the SOC value function, analyze the sub-optimality of the Tweedie-based approximation, and propose the Stein correction mechanism.

\subsection{Value function variational bound}
\label{sec:vf}
% recap
We recall the diffusion posterior $p_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t)$, which is the terminal distribution evolving from the state $\mathbf{x}_t$ under the uncontrolled reverse process $\mathbb{P}$. According to Proposition \ref{theo:cost-func}, we can rewrite the low-density guidance value function as $ V\left(\mathbf{x}, t\right) = -\log \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_T \sim p_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x})}\left[\frac{p_t^{\mathbf{u}}(\mathbf{x})}{p_t(\mathbf{x})}|\mathbf{x}_t = \mathbf{x}\right]$, where $p_t^{\mathbf{u}}(\mathbf{x})=p_t^{1-\alpha(t)}(\mathbf{x})\exp(\beta(t)r(\mathbf{x}_T))$. To make optimization more computationally tractable, we introduce a surrogate objective $\widetilde{V}(\mathbf{x}, t, q)$  with $q \in \mathcal{Q}$, which is the upper bound of the value function:
% proposition 2, variational bound of value function
\begin{proposition}
\label{theo:svf}
    Given a traceable family of distributions $q \in \mathcal{Q}$, the value function in Proposition \ref{theo:cost-func} can be upper bounded:
    \begin{align}
        V(\mathbf{x}, t)  &\leq  \widetilde{V}(\mathbf{x}, t, q) \nonumber\\
        &= - \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_T \sim q_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x})}\left[\log\left(\frac{p_t^{\mathbf{u}}(\mathbf{x})}{p_t(\mathbf{x})}\right)|\mathbf{x}_t = \mathbf{x}\right] + D_{KL}\left(q(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t) \| p(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t)\right)|_{\mathbf{x}_t = \mathbf{x}} \nonumber\\
        &= \alpha(t)\log p_t(\mathbf{x}) - \beta(t) \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_T \sim q_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x})}\left[r(\mathbf{x}_T)|\mathbf{x}_t=\mathbf{x}\right] \nonumber\\
        &\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad + D_{KL}\left(q(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t) \| p(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t)\right)|_{\mathbf{x}_t=\mathbf{x}}
    \end{align}
\end{proposition}
% connect tweedie showing suboptimality
The first RHS term corresponds to the part driving samples toward low-density regions. The second term guides samples toward regions with desired properties. When estimating the proposal distribution mean by Tweedie's formula, we recover exactly the training-free diffusion guidance explored in the previous works \cite{chung2023diffusion, moufad2025variational, yu2023freedom, ma2024elucidating, shen2024understanding, rout2025rbmodulation, janati2025mixture, dinh2025representative}, indicating no additional classifier training $r(\cdot)$ on noisy data inputs $\mathbf{x}_t$ required as in the standard classifier-based guidance approaches. The last term serves as a KL regularization that minimizes the divergence between the proposal posterior $q(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t)$ and the true posterior $p(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t)$. This formulation shows that using the Tweedie-based approximate posterior alone is suboptimal, as it ignores the KL regularization and thus fails to ensure proximity to the true diffusion posterior. To address this, we propose a novel diffusion correction mechanism that refines the approximate posterior before drawing samples for guidance.

\subsection{Stein meets Tweedie for surrogate stochastic optimal control}
\label{sec:stein}
% connection with Stein
We consider a set of samples (or particles) residing on a noisy diffusion manifold $\mathcal{M}_t$. The optimal control for the surrogate value function $\widetilde{V}(\mathbf{x}, t, q)$ involves one part of minimizing the KL divergence $D_{KL}\left(q(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t) \| p(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t)\right)$. Following the result of Lemma \ref{theo:stein}, this corresponds to evolving the particle set along the KSD's direction on $\mathcal{M}_t$, given as $\phi^*(\mathbf{x}^i_t)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}^j_T \sim q_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t)}[\nabla_{\mathbf{x}^j_t}\log p(\mathbf{x}^j_T|\mathbf{x}_t^j)k(\mathbf{x}^i_T, \mathbf{x}^j_T) + \nabla_{\mathbf{x}^j_t}k(\mathbf{x}^i_T, \mathbf{x}^j_T)]$.  However, directly computing this direction is memory-intensive, as it requires evaluating numerous Jacobian-vector products on $\partial \mathbf{x}_T^j/\partial \mathbf{x}_t^j$ terms. To address this computation burden, we propose \textit{a back-and-forth correction trick}: first, mapping noisy particles backward to the clean diffusion manifold $\mathcal{M}_T$, applying the Stein correction step there, and then mapping the corrected particles forward to the noisy manifold $\mathcal{M}_t$. Figure \ref{fig:arch} illustrates the key steps involved in this back-and-forth correction process. In the next proposition, we formally present the optimal controller for the surrogate value function:

\begin{algorithm}[t]
   \caption{Stein Diffusion Guidance Algorithm}
   \label{alg:sdg}
\begin{algorithmic}
    \STATE {\bfseries Input}: Score model $s_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_t)$,  batch size $N$, off-the-shelf classifier/ differentiable reward function $r(\mathbf{x}_T)$, stepsize schedule $\epsilon(t)$, annealing schedules $\alpha(t)$ $\beta(t)$, number diffusion steps $T$, forward noising kernel $p_{t|T}(\mathbf{x}_t | \mathbf{x}_T) = \mathcal{N}(\eta(t)\mathbf{x}_T, \gamma^2(t)I)$, diffusion prior $p_0$, diffusion drift $\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x}_t, t)$, diffusion coefficient $\mathbf{\sigma}(t)$. \\
    \STATE {\bfseries Output}: Endpoint samples. \\

    $\mathcal{D}_0  \leftarrow \left\{\mathbf{x}_0^i | \mathbf{x}_0^i \sim p_0, 1   \leqslant i   \leqslant N \right\}$ \COMMENT{initial particle set} 
    \STATE  {\bfseries for} $t \;\; \text{in} \;\; [0, T)$: \\
     /* \textit{Stein correction} */ \\
    \STATE \hspace{10pt} $\mathcal{D}_T \leftarrow \left\{ \mathbf{x}_T^i | \mathbf{x}_T^i=\frac{\mathbf{x}_t^i +\gamma^2(t) \mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_t^i)}{\eta(t)}, \forall \mathbf{x}_t^i \in \mathcal{D}_t\right\}$ \COMMENT{one-step clean data prediction} \\
    \STATE \hspace{10pt} $\mathbf{x}_T^i = \mathbf{x}_T^i + \epsilon(t) \frac{1}{N} \sum_{\mathbf{x}_t^j,\mathbf{x}_T^j \in \mathcal{D}_t \bigcup \mathcal{D}_T} \left((s_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_T^j) - \eta(t) s_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_t^j))k(\mathbf{x}_T^i, \mathbf{x}_T^j)+\nabla_{\mathbf{x}^j_T}k(\mathbf{x}_T^i, \mathbf{x}_T^j)\right)$
    \STATE \hspace{10pt} $\mathcal{D}_t \leftarrow \left\{\mathbf{x}_i^t | \mathbf{x}_i^t \sim \mathcal{N}(\eta(t)\mathbf{x}_T^i, \gamma^2(t)I), \forall \mathbf{x}_T^i \in \mathcal{D}_T\right\}$ \COMMENT{updated particle set} \\
    /* Low density diffusion guidance */ \\
    \STATE \hspace{10pt} $\mathbf{x}_T^i=\frac{\mathbf{x}_t^i +\gamma^2(t) \mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_t^i)}{\eta(t)}$\\
    \STATE \hspace{10pt} $\mathbf{x}_{t+1}^i = \mathbf{x}_{t}^i -\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x}_t^i, t)+ \sigma(t)^2\left((1-\alpha(t))s_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_t^i) + \beta(t) \nabla_{\mathbf{x}_t^i}r(\mathbf{x}_T^i)\right) + \sigma(t)\mathbf{z},\;\; \mathbf{z} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)$
    \STATE {\bfseries return}: $\left\{\mathbf{x}_T^i, \;\; \forall 1 \leqslant i \leqslant N \right\}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

% % proposition 3, connection with Stein 
\begin{proposition}
    Let $p_{t|T}(\mathbf{x}_t|\mathbf{x}_T)=\mathcal{N}(\eta(t)\mathbf{x}_T, \gamma^2(t)I)$ denote the forward noising kernel, $p_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t)$ the diffusion posterior, $\mathbf{s}_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_t\right)$ the score model, $\mathcal{D}_t = \{\mathbf{x}_t^i\}_{i=0}^N$ the N-sample batch at the $t^{\text{th}}$ reverse timestep, $q_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathcal{D}_t)$ the Tweedie-based proposal distribution conditioned on $\mathcal{D}_t$, and $q^{\epsilon}_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathcal{D}_t)$ its update under a particle-based transform with stepsize $\epsilon(t)$. Then, the optimal control $\widetilde{\mathbf{u}}^*(\mathbf{x}, t)$ for the surrogate value function $\widetilde{V}(\mathbf{x}, t, q)$ decomposes as:
    \begin{align}
        \frac{\widetilde{\mathbf{u}}^*(\mathbf{x}^i_t,t)}{\sigma(t)} &= \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}^j_T \sim q_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathcal{D}_t)}\left[\left(\mathbf{s}_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}^j_T\right)-\eta(t)\mathbf{s}_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}^j_t\right)\right)k(\mathbf{x}^i_T, \mathbf{x}^j_T) + \nabla_{\mathbf{x}^j_T}k(\mathbf{x}^i_T, \mathbf{x}^j_T)| \mathcal{D}_t\right]}_{\textbf{Stein correction on } \mathcal{M}_T} \nonumber\\
        & \oplus \quad \underbrace{-\alpha(t) \mathbf{s}_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}^i_t\right) + \beta(t) \nabla_{\mathbf{x}^i_t}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}^i_T \sim q^{\epsilon}_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathcal{D}_t)}\left[r(\mathbf{x}^i_T)|\mathbf{x}^i_t\right]}_{\textbf{low-density guidance on } \mathcal{M}_t}
    \end{align}
\end{proposition}

where $\oplus$ denotes the concatenation of the two consecutive steps, and $k$ is an RKHS kernel (e.g. RBF kernel). The first term corresponds to the Stein correction on $\mathcal{M}_T$, which refines the initial proposal distribution $q_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathcal{D}_t)$ toward the true diffusion posterior, resulting in an updated distribution $q^{\epsilon}_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathcal{D}_t) \approx p_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t)$. Optimising $-\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_T}D_{KL}\left(q(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathcal{D}_t) \| p(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathbf{x}_t)\right)$ with Lemma \ref{theo:stein}, the KSD's direction can be derived as $\phi^*(\mathbf{x}^i_T)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}^j_T \sim q_{T|t}(\mathbf{x}_T|\mathcal{D}_t)}[\nabla_{\mathbf{x}^j_T}\log p(\mathbf{x}^j_T|\mathbf{x}^j_t)k(\mathbf{x}^i_T, \mathbf{x}^j_T) + \nabla_{\mathbf{x}^j_T}k(\mathbf{x}^i_T, \mathbf{x}^j_T)|\mathcal{D}_t]$, with $\nabla_{\mathbf{x}^j_T}\log p(\mathbf{x}^j_T|\mathbf{x}^j_t) =\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^j_T)-\eta(t)\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^j_t)$.
%non-parametric approach, particle-based, guidance
The Stein correction operates via non-parametric updates, representing a distribution as a set of particles and iteratively transforming them into a new particle set that reflects the updated distribution. After mapping the corrected particles forward to the noisy manifold $\mathcal{M}_t$, the second term performs the guidance step by directing the particles toward low-density regions associated with desired properties. Crucially, our Stein correction ensures robust and accurate guidance even when leveraging off-the-shelf classifiers on samples drawn from approximate posteriors, thereby improving the method’s robustness across a wide range of tasks. We summarize our Stein diffusion guidance in Algorithm \ref{alg:sdg}.

\subsection{Sampling graph permutation-invariant distributions}
\label{sec:graph}
We apply SDG to sample from structured data distributions such as graphs. Given a graph representation $\mathcal{G} = (\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{E})$, where $\mathbf{X}$ and $\mathbf{E}$ denote the node feature matrix and edge matrix, respectively, \citet{jo2022score} models the graph distribution using a system of score-based networks: $s_{\theta}(\mathbf{X}_t, \mathbf{E}_t) \approx \nabla_{\mathbf{X}_t} \log p(\mathbf{X}_t, \mathbf{E}_t)$ and $s_{\phi}(\mathbf{X}_t, \mathbf{E}_t) \approx \nabla_{\mathbf{E}_t} \log p(\mathbf{X}_t, \mathbf{E}_t)$. Here, $s_{\theta}(\cdot)$ and $s_{\phi}(\cdot)$ are permutation-equivariant models that respect the inherent symmetry of graph data. Based on Algorithm \ref{alg:sdg}, we extend the Stein correction on the clean manifold $\mathcal{M}_T$ to handle both node and edge components as follows:
%
\scalebox{.91}{
\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\begin{align}
\mathbf{X}_T^i = \mathbf{X}_T^i + \epsilon_{\mathbf{X}}(t) \frac{1}{N} \sum_{\mathbf{X}_t^j,\mathbf{X}_T^j \in \mathcal{D}_t \bigcup \mathcal{D}_T} \left((s_{\theta}(\mathbf{X}_T^j, \mathbf{E}_T^j) - \eta_{\mathbf{X}}(t) s_{\theta}(\mathbf{X}_t^j, \mathbf{E}_t^j))k(\mathbf{X}_T^i, \mathbf{X}_T^j)+\nabla_{\mathbf{X}^j_T}k(\mathbf{X}_T^i, \mathbf{X}_T^j)\right) \nonumber \\
\mathbf{E}_T^i = \mathbf{E}_T^i + \epsilon_{\mathbf{E}}(t) \frac{1}{N} \sum_{\mathbf{E}_t^j,\mathbf{E}_T^j \in \mathcal{D}_t \bigcup \mathcal{D}_T} \left((s_{\phi}(\mathbf{X}_T^j, \mathbf{E}_T^j) - \eta_{\mathbf{E}}(t) s_{\phi}(\mathbf{X}_t^j, \mathbf{E}_t^j))k(\mathbf{E}_T^i, \mathbf{E}_T^j)+\nabla_{\mathbf{E}^j_T}k(\mathbf{E}_T^i, \mathbf{E}_T^j)\right)
\end{align}
\end{minipage}
}
%
, and then perform low-density guidance on the noisy diffusion manifold $\mathcal{M}_t$:
%
\scalebox{.95}{
\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\begin{align}
&\mathbf{X}_{t+1}^i = \mathbf{X}_{t}^i -\mathbf{b}_{\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{X}_t^i, t)+ \sigma_{\mathbf{X}}(t)^2\left((1-\alpha_{\mathbf{X}}(t))s_{\theta}(\mathbf{X}_t^i, \mathbf{E}_t^i) + \beta_{\mathbf{X}}(t) \nabla_{\mathbf{X}_t^i}r(\mathbf{X}_T^i, \mathbf{E}_T^i)\right) + \sigma_{\mathbf{X}}(t)\mathbf{Z} \nonumber \\
&\mathbf{E}_{t+1}^i = \mathbf{E}_{t}^i -\mathbf{b}_{\mathbf{E}}(\mathbf{E}_t^i, t)+ \sigma_{\mathbf{E}}(t)^2\left((1-\alpha_{\mathbf{E}}(t))s_{\phi}(\mathbf{X}_t^i, \mathbf{E}_t^i) + \beta_{\mathbf{E}}(t) \nabla_{\mathbf{E}_t^i}r(\mathbf{X}_T^i, \mathbf{E}_T^i)\right) + \sigma_{\mathbf{E}}(t)\mathbf{Z}
\end{align}
\end{minipage}
}
%
Here, $\mathbf{X}_T^i$ and $\mathbf{E}_T^i$ are Tweedie-based estimates of the clean node and edge features, given by $\mathbf{X}_T^i = (\mathbf{X}_t^i + \gamma_{\mathbf{X}}(t) s_{\theta}(\mathbf{X}_t^i, \mathbf{E}_t^i))/\eta_{\mathbf{X}}(t)$ and $\mathbf{E}_T^i = (\mathbf{E}_t^i + \gamma_{\mathbf{E}}(t) s_{\phi}(\mathbf{X}_t^i, \mathbf{E}_t^i))/\eta_{\mathbf{E}}(t)$, respectively.

