\subsection{Sampling novel molecular hits}
As shown in Table~\ref{tab:res}, SDG without the Stein correction performs poorly, suggesting that naively applying Tweedie’s formula to approximate diffusion posteriors does not yield accurate representations of the true posterior. As a result, samples drawn from these approximations fail to provide reliable guidance, particularly in low-density regions where score-based models struggle to estimate data scores due to sparse training data. In contrast, SDG substantially enhances guidance by leveraging Stein-corrected samples, leading to improvements of several orders of magnitude. This demonstrates both theoretical and empirical support for our Stein correction mechanism. Moreover, SDG further boosts the performance of the pretrained GDSS model and its classifier-guided variant, MOOD, across different protein targets. Compared to non-diffusion methods, SDG generates more hit compounds for two out of the three target proteins. Figure \ref{fig:ds} compares the docking score distributions on the target proteins. SDG without the Stein correction fails to accurately capture the docking score distributions of the data.  In contrast, SDG more effectively guides molecular samples through low-density regions, shifting the docking-score distributions in the desired direction and enabling the generation of more potential hit compounds. We visualize the docking pose of a novel hit compound example on the fa7 target protein in Figure \ref{fig:hit}.
