\subsection{Reward estimation and off-manifold sampling issues}
%
\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.4\textwidth}
    \centering
    \vspace{-60pt}
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{./figure/radar_5ht1b.pdf}
    \caption{Radar plot showing performance metrics on the 5ht1b target.}
    \label{fig:radar}
    \vspace{-20pt} % Adjust this to control spacing
\end{wrapfigure}
%
\begin{figure}[t]
\centerline{
\includegraphics[width=1.\textwidth]{./figure/genuine_reward.pdf}}
\caption{Sampling dynamics on the 5ht1b protein target. (a) Percentage of molecules satisfying the QED and SA hit conditions; (b) Classifier (reward) scores of predicted clean samples on $\mathcal{M}_T$; (c) Node's model scores on $\mathcal{M}_T$; (d) Edge's model scores on $\mathcal{M}_T$.}
\label{fig:reward}
\end{figure}
%
We study the problem of approximating ground-truth (genuine) rewards using parametric classifier/reward models. In many applications, genuine rewards are computed by non-differentiable oracle functions, which cannot be directly used in standard diffusion guidance methods. Classifiers are trained to learn these genuine rewards and produce approximate (nominal) rewards, serving as differentiable proxies for classifier-based diffusion. However, due to the finite number of training samples, classifiers tend to provide reliable nominal rewards only within the support of the training data distribution. This limitation becomes more severe in low-density sampling problems. \citet{uehara2024fine} first formulated this issue and proposed an entropy-regularized control approach,  which is equivalent to solving a costly stochastic optimal control problem. 
%
In our case study, the absence of regularization leads the reward model to produce highly unreliable estimates of the true reward during the early stages of the sampling process (Figure~\ref{fig:reward}.b), resulting in the generation of novel molecular structures that lack drug-likeness and are difficult to synthesize (Figure~\ref{fig:reward}.a and Figure~\ref{fig:radar}). Additionally, the score models are often misdirected away from the generative manifold (Figure~\ref{fig:reward}.c and~\ref{fig:reward}.d). On the generative manifold $\mathcal{M}_T$, high-density regions are typically associated with low data scores; in non-low-density settings, the score models for predicted clean (denoised) samples tend to move from high-score to low-score regions during the sampling process.
%
SDG provides an efficient regularization strategy via the Stein correction mechanism, which improves reward estimation accuracy (Figure~\ref{fig:reward}.b), promotes the generation of more realistic and synthesizable molecules (Figure~\ref{fig:reward}.a and Figure~\ref{fig:radar}), and preserves sampling within the generative manifold (Figure~\ref{fig:reward}.c and~\ref{fig:reward}.d), particularly in challenging low-density problems.

