As can be seen from Eq.~\eqref{devi_ZV_S} and Figs~\ref{fig:plot_pn_diff_gamma} and~\ref{fig:plot_pn_diff_qm}, it is noteworthy that $\delta_\text{ZV-S}$ vanishes not only in the trivial case $\gamma=1$ ($\tilde{M}_2 =0$), but also in the case $\gamma = 1/(5 + 6 e^2)$ ($\tilde{M}_2 = 4 \left(2+5 e^2+3 e^4 \right)$). If $\gamma$ has this value, the amount of the periapsis shift in the ZV spacetime is the same as the Schwarzschild one up to the 2PN order. This means that the periapsis shift looks as if it is in the Schwarzschild spacetime, even if it is in the ZV spacetime. To resolve this degeneracy, one should examine the orbital period or observe the periapsis shift of another star that has a different eccentricity value. 

A further interesting point is the contribution of oblateness and prolateness to the periapsis shift. In Newtonian gravity, it is known that the oblateness and prolateness of a source contribute to increasing and decreasing the amount of periapsis shift, respectively (see e.g.~\cite{Will:2018}). However, as can be seen from Fig.~\ref{fig:plot_pn_diff}, the oblateness and small prolateness of the ZV spacetime contribute to decreasing and increasing the amount of the periapsis shift, respectively, in contrast to the Newtonian case. Recall that $0<\gamma<1$ ($\tilde{M}_2<0$) corresponds to oblate spacetime and $\gamma>1$ ($\tilde{M}_2>0$) corresponds to prolate spacetime. On the other hand, the large prolateness of the ZV spacetime contributes to decreasing the amount of the periapsis shift, which is consistent with the Newtonian case. The reason why the contributions of oblateness and small prolateness to the periapsis shift are opposite to the Newtonian results is open to further investigation.

