Beyond the classical continuum description, the geometric framework developed here offers promising extensions to quantum systems. The metric induced by the conformal field provides a natural background to investigate Landau level quantization in the presence of topological defects~\cite{Garcia2017}. The spatial localization encoded in the deformation pattern may also model effective confinement potentials for quantum dots or wells in two-dimensional materials~\cite{Bueno2014}. Furthermore, this class of metrics can serve as curved backgrounds for quantum fields, enabling the exploration of curvature-induced phases and geometric couplings in strained media~\cite{Bueno2012}. These perspectives suggest a fertile interface between classical geometry and quantum phenomena in low-dimensional systems.




