\section{Introduction}

In this article we study some particular discrete subgroups of $\SA\bp{\R^{d+1}} = \SL\bp{\R^{d+1}} \ltimes \R^{d+1}$, the group of \emph{special affine transformations of} $\R^{d+1}$. Our interest for such groups comes from previous works in two different settings generalising uniform lattices of $\SO_0(d,1)$.

On one side, \emph{affine deformations of uniform lattices of $\SO_0(d,1)$}, that is subgroups of $\SO_0(d,1) \ltimes \R^{d+1}$ obtained by adding translation parts to uniform lattices, were the focus of the works of Mess \cite{Mess_07,Mess_notes} for $d=2$, as well as the ones of Barbot \cite{Barbot_05} and Bonsante \cite{Bonsante_05} in higher dimension. They proved a correspondence between those subgroups and some flat Lorentzian spacetimes satisfying nice properties. More recent works \cite{Fillastre_Veronelli_16,Bonsante_Fillastre_17, Fillastre_Seppi_19, Barbot_Fillastre_20} have shown that tools from \emph{convex geometry} inside the \emph{Minkowski space $\R^{d,1}$} (on which flat Lorentzian spacetimes are locally modelled) were very useful in the study of those objects, and that their Lorentzian metric was not as important as their \emph{causal} and \emph{affine structures}.

On the other side, the work of Benoist on the rich topic of \emph{divisible convex sets} \cite{Benoist_03,Benoist_04,Benoist_05,Benoist_06} offers an affine generalisation of uniform lattices of $\SO_0(d,1)$: by considering discrete torsion free subgroups of $\SL\bp{\R^{d+1}}$ preserving an open proper convex cone $\C \subset \R^{d+1}$ and with properly discontinuous and cocompact projective action on $\P(\C)$, the projectivisation of the cone. 


\subsection*{Main results}

In this article we introduce and study \emph{affine spacetimes}, which are a natural generalisation of time oriented flat Lorentzian spacetimes. Indeed, we shall define a $(d+1)$-dimensional affine spacetime $(M,\C)$ as a manifold $M$ endowed with both a $(G,X)$-structure where $G = \SL \bp{\R^{d+1}} \ltimes \R^{d+1}$ is the group of orientation and volume preserving affine transformations acting on the affine space $X = \R^{d+1}$, and a \emph{parallel distribution of open proper convex cones} $\C$ in its tangent bundle. Thus, time oriented flat Lorentzian spacetimes are naturally affine spacetimes: they are locally modelled on the Minkowski space $\R^{d,1}$, so they admit a $\bp{\SO_0(d,1) \ltimes \R^{d,1}, \R^{d,1}}$-structure and a natural cone distribution given by the interiors of the future light cones in its tangent bundle. All of the definitions and notions arising purely from the causal structure of Lorentzian spacetimes, for example \emph{Cauchy surfaces} and \emph{global hyperbolicity}, can naturally extend to this new setting (see subsection~\ref{subsec affine spacetimes}).
Hence, affine spacetimes are locally modelled on the \emph{model affine spacetime} $\bp{\A^{d+1}, \C}$: the real affine space $\A^{d+1}$ endowed with a parallel cone structure given by an open proper convex cone $\C$. In that setting, we introduce \emph{$\C$-convex domains} of $\bp{\A^{d+1}, \C}$ as open convex domains which are their own $\C$-future and with Gauss map satisfying some surjectivity condition (see Definition~\ref{def C-convex and C-regular domains}). We then prove the following theorem generalising the work of Mess \cite{Mess_07,Mess_notes}, Barbot \cite{Barbot_05}, and Bonsante \cite{Bonsante_05}.

\begin{theorem}
\label{Theorem Intro domain}
Let $\Gamma_\tau < \SL \bp{\R^{d+1}} \ltimes \R^{d+1}$ be a discrete torsion-free group obtained by adding translations part to a subgroup $\Gamma < \SL\bp{\R^{d+1}}$ dividing an open proper convex cone $\C \subset \R^{d+1}$, i.e. such that the projective action of $\Gamma$ on $\mathbb{P}(\C)$ is properly discontinuous and cocompact. There exists a unique maximal $\Gamma_\tau$-invariant $\C$-convex domain $D_\tau \subset \R^{d+1}$. It satisfies the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item \label{theo11} The action of $\Gamma_\tau$ on $D_\tau$ is free and properly discontinuous, and there is a homeomorphism
\begin{equation*}
D_\tau /\Gamma_\tau \simeq \mathbb{P}(\C)/\Gamma \times \R.
\end{equation*}
\item \label{theo12} The affine spacetime $\bp{D_\tau / \Gamma_\tau,\C}$ is a maximal globally hyperbolic spacetime admitting a locally uniformly convex and compact Cauchy surface (a MGHCC affine spacetime).
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark*}
The existence and uniqueness of the maximal $\Gamma_\tau$-invariant $\C$-convex domain $D_\tau$, on which $\Gamma_\tau$ acts properly discontinuously was already proved in the work of Choi \cite[Theorems 4.3.1 and 4.3.7]{Choi_25} in a more general setting and with different language and approach. In dimension $2+1$, that, as well as the topology of the quotient $D_\tau /\Gamma_\tau$, was also independently shown by Nie and Seppi \cite[Theorem~A]{Nie_Seppi_23}. The topology of the quotient in higher dimension, the notion of affine spacetime, the whole point \ref{theo12}, as well as Theorems~\ref{Theorem Intro dev} and \ref{Theorem Intro cosmological time} below are completely new to the best of our knowledge. 
\end{remark*}

Notice that Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro domain} can also be applied using the cone $-\C$, which is also divisible by $\Gamma$, in order to produce another couple of domain and affine spacetime.


Applying Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro domain} in the particular case where $\Gamma < \SO_0(d,1)$ is the holonomy of a closed hyperbolic manifold and $\C$ is the future light cone in the Minkowski space $\R^{d,1}$, we recover some of the works of Mess \cite{Mess_07,Mess_notes}, Barbot \cite{Barbot_05}, and Bonsante \cite{Bonsante_05}.

In order to complete our generalisation of the correspondence of Mess, Barbot, and Bonsante, we also show the following theorem, stating that these examples of MGHCC affine spacetimes given by Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro domain} happen to be the only ones.

\begin{theorem}
\label{Theorem Intro dev}
Let $(M,\C^M)$ be MGHCC affine spacetime. Any pair $\bp{\dev,\rho}$ of a developing map
\begin{equation*}
\dev: \widetilde{M}~\longrightarrow~\R^{d+1},
\end{equation*}
and a holonomy map 
\begin{equation*}
\rho: \pi_1M~\longrightarrow~\SL \bp{\R^{d+1}} \ltimes \R^{d+1},
\end{equation*}
satisfies the following:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\rho$ is a group isomorphism onto its image $ \rho(\pi_1M)$, 
\item $\rho(\pi_1M)$ is an affine deformation $\Gamma_\tau$ of its linear part $\Gamma < \SL \bp{\R^{d+1}}$ which divides the cone $\C \coloneqq \dif \, \dev(\C^M)$,
\item $\dev$ is an isomorphism of affine spacetimes onto its image which is $(D_\tau,\C)$, where $D_\tau $, the domain associated with $\Gamma_\tau \coloneqq \rho(\pi_1M)$ and $\C$, is given by Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro domain}.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\subsection*{Affine cosmological time}
Affine spacetimes significantly differ from flat Lorentzian spaces as their cone distribution does not come as the distribution of isotropic cones for a Lorentzian metric. Nevertheless, similarly to recent works of Kunziger and Clemens \cite{Kunziger_Clemens_18}, Papadopoulos and Yamada \cite{Papadopoulos_Yamada_19}, or Buro \cite{Buro_23}, based on the axiomatisation of \emph{timelike spaces} by Busemann \cite{Busemann_67}, one can introduce a way to define length on the family of causal curves, satisfying, among other good properties, a \emph{time inequality}. Then, a \emph{cosmological time function} (see Section~\ref{sec Cosmological time on C-convex domains}) can be defined similarly to the usual Lorentzian one \cite{Andersson_Galloway_Howard_98}. We then prove the following fact concerning $\C$-convex domain of the model affine spacetime $\bp{\R^{d+1},\C}$.

\begin{theorem}
\label{Theorem Intro cosmological time}
Let $\C$ be an open proper convex cone in $\R^{d+1}$ with vertex at the origin. Every $\C$-convex domain $K$ in $\bp{\R^{d+1},\C}$ admits a concave $C^1$ cosmological time function $\mathcal{T}_K: K~\longrightarrow~\R^*_+$ whose level sets are $C^1$ convex Cauchy surfaces of $(K,\C)$ foliating $K$. 
Moreover, $\mathcal{T}_K$ is invariant by any transformation of $\SA\bp{\R^{d+1}}$ preserving $K$.
\end{theorem}

The cosmological time and its foliation will be a central tool in order to study topological aspects of the action of $\Gamma_\tau$ on $D_\tau$ and prove Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro domain}. 

The cosmological time on a domain $D_\tau$ from Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro domain} is thus $\Gamma_\tau$-invariant and descends to the quotient, so that the affine spacetime $\bp{D_\tau / \Gamma_\tau,\C}$ is foliated by the level hypersurfaces of its cosmological time, which are $C^1$ embedded convex Cauchy surfaces. Using Theorems~\ref{Theorem Intro domain},~\ref{Theorem Intro dev}, and~\ref{Theorem Intro cosmological time}, that gives the following.

\begin{corollary}
\label{Corollary Intro MGHCC cosmological time}
Any MGHCC affine spacetime $(M,\C)$ is naturally endowed with a $C^1$ locally concave cosmological time $\mathcal{T}$. The level hypersurfaces of this cosmological time are $C^1$ $\C$-spacelike convex Cauchy surfaces foliating $M$.
\end{corollary}

In this article we choose to use \emph{Cheng--Yau affine spheres} \cite{Cheng_Yau_86} in order to normalise timelike vectors, measure lengths of timelike curves, and produce the cosmological time function from Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro cosmological time}. That choice is partially arbitrary: one could use any other known locally convex hypersurfaces invariant by automorphisms of the cone and with similar properties (strict convexity, smoothness, asymptotic behaviour), for instance hypersurfaces given by the level sets of the \emph{Vinberg functional} \cite{Vinberg_63,Goldman_22}. Still, one can notice that in the case of a \emph{homogeneous convex cone} (with automorphism group acting transitively), which happens to be the setting of the Koszul--Vinberg theory \cite{Koszul_62,Vinberg_63}, up to homothety there is a unique hypersurface satisfying those properties (hence the affine sphere and the Vinberg hypersurface coincide). One of the aims of this article is thus to show how useful and natural is the use of affine spheres and the theory of \emph{affine differential geometry} in that setting. 

On the one side, that choice to normalise vectors through affine spheres is an actual generalisation of the Lorentzian case: in any tangent space of a Lorentzian spacetime, timelike vectors are normalised by the hyperboloid which is a Cheng--Yau affine sphere associated with the isotropic cone of the Lorentzian metric. The work of Minguzzi \cite{Minguzzi_17} actually gives a physical interpretation to that choice.
Moreover, affine spheres have the advantage to behave well with the duality and convexity tools that were used in the Lorentzian case. Indeed, fundamental tools from convex analysis, such as \emph{duality, subdifferentials} or the \emph{Legendre--Fenchel transform}, were already used by Cheng and Yau \cite{Cheng_Yau_77,Cheng_Yau_86} in order to prove their fundamental theorem about existence of complete hyperbolic affine sphere asymptotic to any given proper convex cone in the affine space. 

On the other side, tools from convex analysis and convex bodies theory \cite{Schneider_93,Fillastre_13} have been successfully used in the study of flat Lorentzian spacetimes \cite{Fillastre_Veronelli_16,Bonsante_Fillastre_17}, through the description and use of \emph{co-Minkowski geometry} \cite{Fillastre_Seppi_19,Barbot_Fillastre_20}, also known as \emph{‘‘half-pipe'' geometry} \cite{Danciger_13}. Those tools rely mainly on the study of variations of the volume of convex bodies and properties of their \emph{Gauss map}. The affine space, which is devoid of metrics but happens to be endowed with a volume form, is a perfect setting to extend those works, and affine spheres are natural candidates to be images of Gauss maps. Substituting those hyperbolic affine spheres for the sphere or the hyperboloid in, respectively, Euclidean or Minkowski convex geometry \cite{Schneider_93,Fillastre_13}, one can also define and study an affine version of the Minkowski problem, generalising results in the Minkowski space \cite{Bonsante_Fillastre_17}, but also results about surfaces with constant affine Gaussian curvature \cite{Labourie_07,Nie_Seppi_22}. We leave this problem for future investigation \cite{Ablondi_prep}.

\subsection*{Links with higher Teichmüller theory}

Let us first focus on the particularly interesting case where $d=2$. Let $S_g$ be a closed oriented and connected surface of genus $g \geq 2$. We consider $\Pg$ the \emph{moduli space of convex projective structures on $S_g$}. By the work of Choi and Goldman \cite{Choi_Goldman_93}, $\Pg$ is identified with the \emph{Hitchin component} \cite{Hitchin_92} of the representation space of $\pi_1\bp{S_g}$ into $\SL\bp{\R^3}$ and it can be described as 
\begin{equation*}
\Pg = \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathrm{div}} \Bp{\pi_1\bp{S_g}, \SL\bp{\R^3}} \Big/\SL\bp{\R^3},
\end{equation*}
where the quotient is by conjugation and $\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathrm{div}} \bp{\pi_1\bp{S_g}, \SL\bp{\R^3}}$ is the set of faithful group representations $\rho \in \mathrm{Hom}\bp{\pi_1\bp{S_g} , \SL\bp{\R^3}}$ such that there is an open proper convex cone $\C_\rho \subset \R^3$ divisible by $\rho\bp{\pi_1\bp{S_g} }$. Then, similarly, $\Pgaff$, the \emph{moduli space of affine deformations of convex projective structures on $S_g$}, can be described as 
\begin{equation*}
\Pgaff = \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathrm{div}} \Bp{\pi_1\bp{S_g} , \SL\bp{\R^3} \ltimes \R^3} \Big/ \Bp{\SL\bp{\R^3} \ltimes \R^3 },
\end{equation*}
where the quotient is by conjugation and $\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathrm{div}} \bp{\pi_1\bp{S_g} , \SL\bp{\R^3} \ltimes \R^3}$ is the set of group representations $\rho_\tau \in \mathrm{Hom}\bp{\pi_1\bp{S_g} , \SL\bp{\R^3} \ltimes \R^3}$ with linear part $\rho \in \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathrm{div}}\bp{\pi_1\bp{S_g} , \SL\bp{\R^3}}$.

Labourie \cite{Labourie_07} and Loftin \cite{Loftin_01} both independently used Choi--Goldman theorem to get a holomorphic parametrisation of those Hitchin components. More recently, Nie and Seppi \cite{Nie_Seppi_22} have highlighted how $\Pgaff$ is naturally a topological vector bundle of rank $6g-6$ on the $(16g-16)$-dimensional Hitchin component given by the natural projection 
\begin{equation*}
\namelessfunction{\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathrm{div}} \Bp{\pi_1\bp{S_g} , \SL\bp{\R^3} \ltimes \R^3}}{\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathrm{div}} \Bp{\pi_1\bp{S_g} , \SL\bp{\R^3}}}{\rho_\tau}{\rho}
\end{equation*}
Through the special identification between the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{so}(2,1)$ and the Minkowski space $\R^{2,1}$, affine deformations of Fuchsian subgroups of $\SO_0(2,1)$ can be seen as first order derivatives of variation of hyperbolic structures on $S_g$. Then the part of $\Pgaff$ over the Teichmüller space $\mathcal{T}_g \subset \Pg$, studied by Mess \cite{Mess_07}, can be identified with the tangent bundle of $\mathcal{T}_g$. Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro domain} and Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro dev} thus give a correspondence between each element of $\Pgaff$ and two (maybe equal) isomorphisms classes of $(2+1)$-dimensional MGHCC affine spacetimes with Cauchy surfaces homeomorphic to $S_g$. In the Minkowski case \cite{Mess_07,Bonsante_05}, one can make this correspondence one to one by arbitrarily choosing a time orientation on the Minkowski space. In the general case, as the divisible cones vary, one cannot make such a simple choice.

In \cite[Section 7]{Labourie_07}, Labourie explicitly gives two different parametrisations of the moduli space $\Pgaff$, as well as some symmetries on it. Using the correspondence presented in this article, and a geometric study of the $(2+1)$-dimensional MGHCC affine spacetimes associated to elements of $\Pgaff$, we can hope for new geometric counterparts and explanations to Labourie's work (similarly to previous works on the Teichmüller case \cite{Bonsante_Seppi_16,Barbot_Fillastre_20}).

The work of Benoist \cite{Benoist_05} also has highlighted that divisibility gives a geometric interpretation to some connected components of higher moduli spaces of representations (higher in the sense that they have images in higher rank Lie groups, such as $\SL\bp{\R^{d+1}}$, but also as they concern representations of fundamental groups of manifolds of higher dimension $d \geq 3$). Letting $\Gamma_0$ be a finitely generated group, he considered $\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathrm{div}} \bp{\Gamma_0, \SL\bp{\R^{d+1}}}$ the set of faithful group representation $\rho \in \mathrm{Hom}\bp{\Gamma_0, \SL\bp{\R^{d+1}}}$ such that there is an open proper convex cone $\C_\rho \subset \R^{d+1}$ divisible by $\rho(\Gamma_0)$. He proved that if $\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathrm{div}} \bp{\Gamma_0, \SL\bp{\R^{d+1}}}$ admits a strongly irreducible representation then it consists of a union of connected components of $\mathrm{Hom} \bp{\Gamma_0, \SL\bp{\R^{d+1}}}$.

Adopting a projective approach, we shall also highlight that the discrete subgroups $\Gamma_\tau$ considered in Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro domain} have a \emph{convex cocompact action} on an open subset of $\mathbb{P} \bp{\R^{d+2}}$ (see Appendix~\ref{sec Projective approach}). When $\Gamma$ is \emph{word hyperbolic}, those are moreover \emph{strongly convex cocompact} in the sense of Danciger, Guéritaud, and Kassel \cite{DGK_17}, and thus, following from their work, are also \emph{projective Anosov}.

It is compelling to try to link our approach to the recent work of Bobb and Farre \cite{Bobb_Farre_24} concerning the generalisation of the part of the Mess correspondence concerning measured geodesic laminations. It should be possible to make those works meet using convex geometry and area measures associated with $\C$-convex domain, similarly to what was done in the Minkowski case \cite{Fillastre_Veronelli_16,Bonsante_Fillastre_17,Barbot_Fillastre_20}.

\subsection*{Organisation of the article} 

We first introduce the definition of an affine spacetime in Section~\ref{Sec Affine spacetimes}. We then give a few useful results and definitions from affine differential geometry and convex analysis in Section~\ref{sec Backgrounds}. Section~\ref{sec About C-convex domains} is dedicated to the study of the special case of the model affine spacetime $\bp{\A^{d+1}, \C}$: the real affine space $\A^{d+1}$ endowed with a parallel cone structure given by an open proper convex cone $\C$. In Section~\ref{sec Affine deformations of divisible convex cones}, we focus on the case where $\C$ is divisible by a subgroup $\Gamma$ of $\SL \bp{\V^{d+1}}$ the group of special linear transformations of the vector space $\V^{d+1}$. In that setting, we build for any affine deformation of $\Gamma$ the maximal invariant domain $D_\tau$ from Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro domain}. Within Section~\ref{sec Cosmological time on C-convex domains}, we introduce and study a cosmological time function on $\C$-convex domains, as well as giving it a geometric interpretation in the context of pseudo-Finsler geometry. That allows us, in Section~\ref{sec Quotient of the maximal domain}, to study the spacetime structure of the quotients $D_\tau / \Gamma_\tau$ and prove point~\ref{theo11} and the GHCC part of point~\ref{theo12} of Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro domain}, as well as Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro cosmological time}. Finally, Section~\ref{sec Correspondence between affine deformations of dividing groups and affine spacetimes} consists of the proofs of the maximality part of point~\ref{theo12} in Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro domain} and of Theorem~\ref{Theorem Intro dev}, thus extending the Mess correspondence. In addition, Appendix~\ref{sec Examples of affine deformations} gives examples in any dimension of groups dividing an open proper convex domains not projectively equivalent to an ellipse (giving the Klein ball model of the hyperbolic space), and admitting non-trivial affine deformations, while Appendix~\ref{sec Projective approach} links the work of this article to the notion of convex cocompact representation through a more projective approach to affine deformations.

