\section{Affine spacetimes}
\label{Sec Affine spacetimes}

Let us first introduce and study one of the central notions of this work: affine spacetime.

\subsection{Equiaffine manifolds}
\label{subsec Equiaffine manifolds}

Let $d \geq 2$ be an integer. We shall mostly work with $\R^{d+1}$ and use different notations to denote that space, depending on the underlying structures under consideration:
\begin{itemize}
\item let $\V^{d+1}$ denote the $(d+1)$-dimensional real vector space endowed with the volume form given by the determinant map $\det: \bigwedge^{d+1} \V^{d+1}~\longrightarrow~\R$,
\item let $\A^{d+1}$ denote the $(d+1)$-dimensional real affine space with associated vector space $\V^{d+1}$, it is endowed with the volume form from $\V^{d+1}$, the flat affine connection $D$ and an orientation. 
\end{itemize} 
Throughout the whole article, as long as we have not made a choice of parametrisation we shall use the notations $\A^{d+1}$ and $\V^{d+1}$ rather than $\R^{d+1}$.

A \emph{real equiaffine structure}, or simply \emph{equiaffine structure}, on a smooth manifold $M$ of dimension $d+1$, consists of an atlas with values in the affine space $\A^{d+1}$, such that the transition maps are restrictions of orientation and volume preserving affine transformations of $\A^{d+1}$. In the language of $(G, X)$-structures, an equiaffine structure on $M$ is a $\bp{\SA\bp{\A^{d+1}},\A^{d+1}}$-structure, where $\SA\bp{\A^{d+1}}$ is the group of special affine transformations of $\A^{d+1}$, namely:
\begin{equation*}
\SA\bp{\A^{d+1}}= \SL \bp{\V^{d+1}} \ltimes \V^{d+1}.
\end{equation*}
A manifold endowed with an equiaffine structure is called an \emph{equiaffine manifold}. It is naturally endowed with an orientation, a flat torsion-free affine connection (coming from the flat connection on $\A^{d+1}$) and a volume form parallel under that connection.

If $M$ is an equiaffine manifold with universal cover $\widetilde{M}$, the theory of $(G, X)$-structures tells us that one can fix a \emph{developing map} 
\begin{equation*}
\dev: \widetilde{M}~\longrightarrow~\A^{d+1}
\end{equation*}
and a \emph{holonomy representation}
\begin{equation*}
 \rho: \pi_1 M~\longrightarrow~\SA\bp{\A^{d+1}},
\end{equation*}
satisfying the following:
\begin{itemize}
\item the developing map $\dev$ is $\rho$-equivariant, in the sense that for all $p \in \widetilde{M}$ and $\gamma \in \pi_1M$,
\begin{equation*}
 \dev(\gamma \cdot p) = \rho(\gamma) \, \dev(p),
\end{equation*}
\item the developing map $\dev$ is a local diffeomorphism preserving the equiaffine structure, i.e. on each chart of the equiaffine structure of $\widetilde{M}$, $\dev$ is an element of $\SA \bp{\A^{d+1}}$.
\end{itemize}
Moreover, given two such pairs $(\dev,\rho)$ and $(\dev',\rho')$ there exists a unique $g \in \SA \bp{\A^{d+1}}$ such that
\begin{equation*}
 \dev' = g \, \dev \quad \textup{and} \quad \rho' = g \, \rho \, g^{-1}.
\end{equation*}


Equivalently, without the language of $(G, X)$-structures, an equiaffine manifold is just a triplet $(M, D, \omega)$ where $M$ is an orientable manifold endowed with a flat torsion-free affine connection $D$ and a volume form $\omega$ such that $D \omega = 0$.

\subsection{Cone structure and causality}


\begin{definition}[Convex cone structure]
A \emph{convex cone structure $\C$} on a smooth connected manifold $M$ is a field of cones $\left\lbrace \C_p \st p \in M \right\rbrace$, where for every point $p \in M$, $\C_p$ is an \emph{open proper} (i.e. non-empty and having closure $\overline{\C}$ containing no entire line) \emph{convex cone} in $\mathrm{T}_pM$ with vertex at the origin.
\end{definition}

The following notions are classical in Lorentzian geometry. Noticing that they rely only on the existence of a light cone distribution in the tangent bundle and not on the metric, we can naturally extend those to our setting.

\begin{definition}[$\C$-spacelike, $\C$-null and $\C$-timelike vector]
Let $M$ be a smooth connected manifold endowed with a convex cone structure $\C$. A non-zero vector $v \in \mathrm{T}_p M$ is said to be:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{$\C$-spacelike} if $v \notin \overline{\C_p} \cup \overline{(-\C_p)}$,
\item \emph{$\C$-null} if $v \in \partial \C_p \cup \partial (-\C_p)$,
\item \emph{$\C$-timelike} if $v \in \C_p \cup (-\C_p)$.
\end{itemize}
A $\C$-null or $\C$-timelike vector $v \in \mathrm{T}_p M$ is said to be \emph{$\C$-future directed} if $v \in \overline{\C_p}$ and \emph{past directed} if $v \in \overline{(-\C_p)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$\C$-spacelike and $\C$-null hyperplanes]
\label{def C-sp C-n hyperplanes}
Let $\C \subseteq \V^{d+1}$ be an open proper convex cone with vertex at the origin.

A linear hyperplane of $\V^{d+1}$ is said to be \emph{$\C$-spacelike} if it only intersect $\overline{\C}$ at its vertex $0$ (see Figure~\ref{Hyperplanes figure}).

A linear hyperplane of $\V^{d+1}$ is said to be \emph{$\C$-null} if it is a supporting hyperplane of $\C$ at point which is not the vertex of $\C$ (see Figure~\ref{Hyperplanes figure}). 

An affine hyperplane of $\A^{d+1}$ is said to be respectively \emph{$\C$-null} or \emph{$\C$-spacelike} if it is directed respectively by a $\C$-null or a $\C$-spacelike linear hyperplane. 

The \emph{future side} of a linear $\C$-null or $\C$-spacelike hyperplane is the open half-space it bounds which intersects $\C$. Similarly, the \emph{future side} of an affine $\C$-null or $\C$-spacelike affine hyperplane $H$ is the open half-space it bounds which contains the future cone $p+\C$ of any point $p \in H$.

We denote respectively by $\mathcal{H}_{sp}(\C)$ and $\mathcal{H}_n(\C)$ the sets of $\C$-spacelike and $\C$-null linear hyperplanes of $\V^{d+1}$.
\end{definition}

