\section{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:solution}} 
\label{sec:appendix}

We apply the arguments from~\cite[Section 3.2]{JaberNeumanVoss:23} for the $N$-player price impact game to the present single-player setup. 

First, according to the example in~\cite[Section 3.2]{JaberNeumanVoss:23} with $N=1$, the objective function in~\eqref{eq:objective} can be written in the form of~\eqref{eq:objective_LQversion} by virtue of~\cite[Lemma 3.1]{JaberNeumanVoss:23}. For the sake of clarity, we summarize the required steps here. Indeed, using integration by parts and Fubini's theorem, we can compute
\begin{align*}
\varrho X_T^2 = & \, \varrho x^2 + 2 \varrho \int_0^T X_t \, dX_t = \varrho x^2 - 2 \varrho x \int_0^T u_t \, dt + 2 \varrho \int_0^T \left( \int_0^T u_s 1_{\{ s \leq t \}} \, ds \right) u_t \, dt, \\
\phi \int_0^T X_t^2 dt = & \, \phi \int_0^T \left( x^2 - 2 \int_0^t X_s u_s \, ds \right) dt = \phi x^2 T - 2 \phi \int_0^T \int_0^t \left( x - \int_0^s u_r dr \right) u_s \, ds \, dt \\
= & \,\phi x^2 T - 2 \phi \int_0^T \int_0^t x u_s \, ds \, dt + 2 \phi \int_0^T \int_0^t \left( \int_0^s u_r \, dr \right) u_s \, ds \, dt \\
= & \, \phi x^2 T - 2 \phi \int_0^T x u_s (T-s) \, ds + 2\phi \int_0^T \left( \int_0^T 1_{\{ r \leq s\}} (T-s) u_r \, dr \right) u_s \, ds.
\end{align*}
Consequently, we obtain
\begin{align*}
& \mathbb{E} \left[ \int_0^T \big( S_t - (\boldsymbol{I}_{\theta}(u))_t \big) u_t \, dt - \varepsilon \int_0^T u_t^2 \, dt - \phi \int_0^T X_t^2 \, dt + X_T S_T - \varrho X_T^2 \right] \\
& = \, \mathbb{E} \left[ \int_0^T \big( S_t - (\boldsymbol{I}_{\theta}(u))_t \big) u_t \, dt - \varepsilon \int_0^T u_t^2 \, dt + S_T x - S_T \int_0^T u_t \, dt \right. \\
& \hspace{30pt} - \phi x^2 T + 2 \phi \int_0^T x u_s (T-s) \, ds - 2\phi \int_0^T \left( \int_0^T 1_{\{ r \leq s\}} (T-s) u_r \, dr \right) u_s \, ds \\
& \hspace{30pt} \left. - \varrho x^2 + 2 \varrho x \int_0^T u_t \, dt - 2 \varrho \int_0^T \left( \int_0^T u_s 1_{\{ s \leq t \}} \, ds \right) u_t \, dt \right] \\
& = \, \mathbb{E} \left[ - \langle u, \boldsymbol{I}_{\theta}(u) \rangle - \langle u, \boldsymbol{C}(u) \rangle + \langle b, u \rangle + c \right]
\end{align*}
with $\boldsymbol{C}$, $b$ as defined in~\eqref{eq:C}, \eqref{eq:b}, respectively, and $c = x S_T - \phi x^2 T - \varrho x^2$.

Next, the claim in~\eqref{eq:ustar1} follows from the $N$-player game result provided in~\cite[Theorem 2.8]{JaberNeumanVoss:23} applied to the present single-player setup with objective~\eqref{eq:objective_LQversion} by setting $N=1$, $\boldsymbol{A}_1 = \boldsymbol{A}_3 = 0$, $\boldsymbol{A}_2 = \varepsilon \operatorname{id} + \boldsymbol{I}_{\theta} + \boldsymbol{\tilde{C}}$, $b^0=0$, $b^i = b$, and $c^i = c$ therein. Moreover, note that Assumption 2.4 in~\cite{JaberNeumanVoss:23} on $\boldsymbol{A}_2$ is satisfied because $\boldsymbol{I}_{\theta}$ in~\eqref{eq:operatorI} with propagator kernels of type (I) and (II) is an admissible Volterra operator in the sense of~\cite[Definition 2.2]{JaberNeumanVoss:23} thanks to~\cite[Example 2.5]{JaberNeuman:22}.

