\subsection{Formal proof}
We now begin the formal proof. Throughout, indices are taken modulo 5. Suppose, for contradiction, that there exist five sets \( F_1, F_2, \ldots, F_5 \in \mathcal{F} \) such that for every pair \( (i,j) \in \binom{[5]}{2} \), there is a point \( a_{i,j} \in F_i \cap F_j \) with \( a_{i,j} \notin F_k \) for any \( k \in [5] \setminus \{i,j\} \). Our goal is to construct a planar embedding of the complete graph \( K_5 \), taking the points \( \{a_{i,i+1} : 1 \le i \le 5\} \) as vertices, and then derive a contradiction to Lemma~\ref{lem:Jordan}.



Throughout we use \( \gamma_{i \setminus j} \) to denote a simple curve contained in \( F_i \setminus F_j \). Note that for each pair $(i,j)\in\binom{[5]}{2}$, \( F_i \setminus F_j \) is connected by the non-piercing property. Therefore for each $i\in [5]$, there exists a simple curve \( \gamma_{i \setminus (i+2)} \subseteq F_i \setminus F_{i+2} \) connecting the points \( a_{i-1,i} \) and \( a_{i,i+1} \). 

The most crucial part in this proof is that,  for each \( i \in [5] \) (indices modulo 5), we can select two additional simple curves: \( \gamma_{i+1 \setminus (i+2)}^{(1)} \subseteq F_{i+1} \setminus F_{i+2} \), connecting \( a_{i,i+1} \) and \( a_{i+1,i+3} \), and \( \gamma_{i+1 \setminus (i+2)}^{(2)} \subseteq F_{i+1} \setminus F_{i+2} \), connecting \( a_{i,i+1} \) and \( a_{i+4,i} \). 

Concatenating \( \gamma_{i+1 \setminus (i+2)} \) and \( \gamma_{i+3 \setminus (i+4)} \) yields a simple path from \( a_{i,i+1} \) to \( a_{i+2,i+3} \). Together with the five curves \( \gamma_{i \setminus (i+2)} \) for \( i \in [5] \), we obtain ten simple curves connecting the ten pairs \( \{a_{i,j} : (i,j) \in \binom{[5]}{2} \} \). These curves collectively define a planar drawing of the complete graph \( K_5 \) with vertex set \( \{a_{i,i+1} : i \in [5]\} \). 

Next we show one of the key advantage of our method to construct this planar drawing of $K_{5}$. We say two curves connecting two pair of vertices in this planar drawing of $K_{5}$ are independent if the corresponding four vertices are distinct.
\begin{claim}\label{disjointness}
   For any two independent curves \( e_1 \in \{\gamma_{i \setminus (i+2)} : i \in [5]\} \) and \( e_2 \in \{\gamma_{i \setminus (i+1)}^{(j)}:i\in [5],j\in [2]\}\bigcup\{\gamma_{i \setminus (i+2)}:i\in [5]\} \) do not intersect; that is, their crossing number is zero. 
\end{claim}
\begin{poc}
The first type is the distinct curves $e_{1},e_{2}$ belong to $ \{\gamma_{i \setminus (i+2)} : i \in [5]\}$. 
Fix some $i\in [5]$, $e_1=\gamma_{i\setminus {i+2}}$, then $e_2\in \{\gamma_{i+2\setminus {i+4}},\gamma_{i+3\setminus {i (=i+5\mod 5)}}\}$. 
Note that $\{\gamma_{i+2\setminus {i+4}},\gamma_{i+3\setminus i}\}\cap (F_{i}\setminus F_{i+2})=\emptyset$, 
so  the crossing number of $e_1$ and $e_2$ is zero. 

The second type is that $e_{1}\in  \{\gamma_{i \setminus (i+2)} : i \in [5]\}$ and $e_{2}\in \{\gamma_{i \setminus (i+1)} : i \in [5]\}$. 
Fix some $i\in [5]$, $e_1=\gamma_{i\setminus {i+2}}$,
then $e_2\in \{\gamma_{i+2\setminus {i+3}},\gamma_{i+4\setminus {i (=i+5\mod 5)}}\}$. 
Similarly,  $\{\gamma_{i+2\setminus {i+3}},\gamma_{i+4\setminus i}\}\cap (F_{i}\setminus F_{i+2})=\emptyset$.  
Hence the crossing number of $e_1$ and $e_2$ is zero. This completes the proof.  
 \end{poc}


