\vspace{0.5cm}
%This appendix is for your reference only and will not be included in the published version. 
The appendix explains the relationship between our work and \cite{chen2024parameterized} to clarify their differences and our contributions. Our paper studies the \emph{colorful sum of radii} problem, a generalization of the outlier-robust sum of radii. Each point belongs to a group (or color), and we allow up to $m_i$ unassigned points from group $i$, controlled by a multi-outlier vector $\boldsymbol{m} = (m_1, \ldots, m_\omega)$. This ensures every group is fairly represented, by bounding per-group outliers. In contrast, Chen et al.~\cite{chen2024parameterized} consider a different fairness goal: ensuring that the \emph{selected centers} are fairly distributed among groups. Their model does not involve outliers. 

Our $(2 + \varepsilon)$-approximation algorithm shares structural elements with theirs, as both reduce to variants of the vanilla sum of radii when exponential time in $(k + m)$ is allowed. However, the fairness definitions, problem formulations, and motivations differ significantly. In short, our work addresses a distinct fairness model, and any algorithmic resemblance is due to a natural reduction, not to solving the same problem or extending \cite{chen2024parameterized}. 

More importantly, our $(2 + \varepsilon)$-approximation algorithm can be viewed just as a warm-up. The main contribution of our paper is the subsequent $(7 + \varepsilon)$-approximation algorithm, whose design has nothing in common with \cite{chen2024parameterized}. 


