Incidentally, although $\mC^{(1)}$'s have been given by the relation to $\hp_{ij}^{\col(0)}$  in \eqref{eq:mCs}, they can be computed directly from their expressions 
in terms of $\mJ^{[1]}(\hf^{(0)},\hf^{(0)})$,
see \eqref{eq:mCs1} and \eqref{eq:hJ_exp_eps}.
Indeed, a simple calculation firstly shows that
\begin{subequations}
\begin{equation}
\langle\psi_0\mJ^{[1]}(\hf^{(0)},\hf^{(0)})\rangle=0,
\end{equation}
and thus
\begin{align}
 \mC_j^{(1)}
=&\kappa\langle\psi_j\mJ^{[1]}(\hf^{(0)},\hf^{(0)})\rangle 
=\kappa\langle\hC_j\mJ^{[1]}(\hf^{(0)},\hf^{(0)})\rangle \\
=&-\frac{\kappa}{3}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}\mG \hr
  \langle \hC_j \hf^{(0)}
  \Big[ \frac{3}{5}\hC_i(\frac{\hbC^2}{\hT}-\frac52)
  \frac{\partial}{\partial x_i}\ln\hT 
 +\hC_i\frac{\partial\ln(\hr^2\mG\hT)}{\partial x_i}\Big] \rangle \notag\\
=&-\frac{\kappa}{6}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}
   \frac{\partial(\hr^2\mG\hT)}{\partial x_j},  \notag
\end{align}
\begin{align}
 \mC_4^{(1)}
=\kappa\langle\psi_4\mJ^{[1]}&(\hf^{(0)},\hf^{(0)})\rangle 
=\kappa\langle(\hbC^2+2\hv_j\hC_j)\mJ^{[1]}(\hf^{(0)},\hf^{(0)})\rangle \\
=&2\hv_j\mC_j^{(1)}
  -\frac{4}{15}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}\kappa\mG \hr 
   \langle \hbC^2 \frac{\hC_i\hC_j}{\hT} \hf^{(0)}\rangle
   \frac{\partial\hv_j}{\partial x_i}   \notag\\
=&-\frac{\kappa}{3}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}\hv_j
   \frac{\partial(\hr^2\mG\hT)}{\partial x_j}
  -\frac{\kappa}{3}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}\mG 
   \hr^2\hT\frac{\partial\hv_j}{\partial x_j}\notag\\
=&-\frac{\kappa}{3}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}
   \frac{\partial}{\partial x_j}\Big(\hr^2\mG\hT\hv_j\Big). \notag
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
%
\noindent
The above derivation is much simpler than that from $\hp_{ij}^{\col}$ and $\hq_i^{\col}$. However, it requires the information on the expansion of $\hf$ by one-order higher than that of $\hp_{ij}^{\col}$ and $\hq_i^{\col}$. 
Therefore, it is advantageous to take the course
through $\hp_{ij}^{\col}$ and $\hq_i^{\col}$
when proceeding to higher order calculations. 



